 "overlaps": [
  {
   "part": "svc_sold (services sold to Russia, uncovered.json services)",
   "overlap": "None for labour remittances. svc_sold is EBOPS services trade (Eurostat bop_its6_det / BaTiS). Compensation of employees is primary income and personal transfers are secondary income: neither is in services trade.",
   "caution": "Relocants' spending in the host country (rent, travel) sits in travel credits, so GEO/ARM svc_sold jumps in 2022 (GEO 0.25 -> 0.95) already carry part of the relocant money that this file leaves out. Keep relocant_eur_bn out to avoid counting it twice; the owner may also want to check whether relocants' travel spending should stay in svc_sold under the same rule."
  },
  {
   "part": "payment_channels.json",
   "overlap": "None. It lists the 2022 remittance surge as recorded but not counted (relocants, capital flight). Agent margins are fees, not wages."
  },
  {
   "part": "company_flows.json (profits remitted by Russian-owned companies)",
   "overlap": "None: investment income, not compensation of employees."
  },
  {
   "part": "refugees_net.json",
   "overlap": "None: it counts no remittances (and none from refugees to Ukraine)."
  },
  {
   "part": "from_rus / to_rus goods trade and re-exports",
   "overlap": "Possible through the non-labour part only. In ARM, KGZ and UZB some payments for goods go through individuals' transfers. These sit in relocant_eur_bn (non-labour) and are not counted, so no double count."
  },
  {
   "part": "uncovered.json hidden_channels",
   "overlap": "None: gold and crypto only."
  },
  {
   "part": "site/data.json fields",
   "overlap": "No field holds remittances or compensation of employees (checked: no \"remit\" key; svc_sold, company, private, people, hub are other flows)."
  }
 ],
 "notes": [
  "Labour remittances above the expected level count toward Russia (money earned from Russia), like services sold to Russia; below it they count toward Ukraine at product_when_less.",
  "relocant_eur_bn = total minus labour: relocants, capital moved by migrants and Russians, Russians' own-account transfers, and trade payments through individuals. Left out by owner decision.",
  "Uzbekistan and Tajikistan dominate. Their gaps are driven by Russian rouble wages (war labour shortage) and, partly, by a shift of migrants from cash to formal card/P2P channels; the latter is not real extra income and is why extra is 0.30-0.80, not ~0.9.",
  "Coercion context only (not counted): after the March 2024 Crocus attack Russia tightened migrant rules (raids, patent costs up in 2026, registry of \"controlled persons\"); Central Asian migrants were pressed to sign military contracts (citizenship-for-service), and Nepalis, Indians, Cubans and others were recruited as soldiers. Military pay is not in these files.",
  "Other receivers checked: Ukraine (not a map country for this flow); T\u00fcrkiye, China, Vietnam, Israel, Serbia, Germany, UAE get little labour income from Russia (< EUR 0.1bn a year, or relocant flows only). North Korea's workers in Russia (est. 10,000-15,000 since 2024) are a state labour export, not modelled here.",
  "Bank of Russia country data were not available for 2022-2025 (publication paused; resumed only for totals in Aug 2026), so all figures come from receiving countries."
 ]
